<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Confederation of the Equator</h1><p class="st-meta">1824 CE – 1824 CE · capital Recife</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A republican revolt in northeastern Brazil against Emperor Pedro I's authoritarian constitution, crushed within months in 1824.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1823 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-i-of-brazil">Pedro I</a> dissolved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil's</a> Constituent Assembly, and in 1824 he imposed a constitution that gave the emperor a moderating power. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/captaincy-of-pernambuco">Pernambuco</a>, already the home of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-pernambuco">Republic of Pernambuco</a>, rejected it. On 2 July 1824 its president, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-de-carvalho-pais-de-andrade">Manuel de Carvalho Pais de Andrade</a>, proclaimed the Confederation of the Equator in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recife">Recife</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The confederation invited the northeastern provinces to form a federal republic modelled on the Colombian constitution, and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraiba">Paraíba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande-do-norte">Rio Grande do Norte</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceara">Ceará</a> joined. The friar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frei-caneca">Joaquim do Amor Divino Caneca</a>, known as Frei Caneca, was its leading ideologue through his newspaper. Divisions over ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> weakened the movement.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Imperial forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-lima-e-silva">Francisco de Lima e Silva</a> and a fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-cochrane-10th-earl-of-dundonald">Lord Cochrane</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recife">Recife</a> in September 1824, and resistance ended by November. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frei-caneca">Frei Caneca</a> and other leaders were executed in 1825, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/captaincy-of-pernambuco">Pernambuco</a> was punished by losing territory.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/confederation-of-the-equator.jpg" alt="Confederation of the Equator" width="320" height="135"><figcaption>Image: Leandro Martins · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Confederacao_equador_1824_exercito_imperial.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1824 CE – 1824 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Recife</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Manuel de Carvalho Pais de Andrade</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Confederação do Equador</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1824&amp;focus=confederation-of-the-equator" aria-label="Confederation of the Equator on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3635 2489 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Confederation of the Equator lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3955 2745L3846 3279"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3846 3279h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3955 2745h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3955 2745h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1824&amp;focus=confederation-of-the-equator">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
